  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `{cite}` of `requirements.authoring.usage_storage` on `src/authoring/usage.rs#struct[UsageLog]` is the right role.\n\n- **Not a test.** `UsageLog` is a type, not a test or test helper.\n- **Not an impl root.** `UsageLog` is the in-memory handle for one usage record. It holds the serialized `UsageRecord` and the `path` of the per-run JSON file. It also holds runtime-only state: `started`, `seen`, `active_request` and `first_request_finished`. The record's fields (version, run, project root, subject, outcome, determination, `evidence_output`, `evidence_written`) are defined on `UsageRecord`. The `<state-root>/authoring/usage/<run-id>.json` path is built in `impl[UsageLog]/fn[start]`. Items marked `{impl}` for `usage_storage` already reach `UsageLog` by using it: `src/authoring/mod.rs#impl[PendingAssessment]/fn[persisted]`, `fn[failed]`, `fn[prepare_assessment]` and `fn[author_evidence_with_output]`. They use it as `self.usage`, and `fn[start]` constructs it. The struct is therefore not a root and needs no `{impl}`.\n- **Cite is justified.** The struct's `record` and `path` fields embody the requirement's contract: a separate JSON record per run, stored at a path, and kept apart from source text. A change to that text, such as the record's storage form or location, could require changing the struct. That makes `{cite}` appropriate.\n- **No redundancy.** The item has no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to the same requirement, so the citation is not redundant.",
